Z||000003||87FieroGTx||11-15-2001||10:41 AM|||| The main reason for me is the very low weight, It weighs the same as an I4 Duke and that would translate into much better handling.<P> "IF" I can find one I'm going to swap it in than have WCF try to turbo it.<P> 300-380hp (Turbocharged)<BR> Better handling<BR> Low weight<BR> 7,000rpm redline<BR> Great sound<P> If I can't find one I'll probably go 3.4 TDC or N* OR maybe custom build a 9,000rpm aluminum block V6  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">LoL<P><BR>  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Master_Sushi||11-15-2001||11:05 AM||||I've also been wondering why more people don't use the 3.4dohc. The engine can really be moded out, I've herd it was detuned from the factory. Got to love the high rpm powerband also! Ever hear one of these engines wound out? They have a really nice sound, much better then any pushrod IMHO.<P>Does anyone on this forum have one? I'd love to see pics!||||reg||6
Z||000005||87FieroGTx||11-15-2001||11:20 AM|||| The 3.5 Liter is the latest in GM's stable of engines. It was designed to take over for the 3.4 Twin Duel Cam that was discontinued. The 3.5 Liters design was based around the 4.6 liter North-Star V-8. <BR>Here is what GM says about the new 3.5 Liter V6 Engine <BR>The state-of-the-art 24-valve powerplant uses the latest in overhead cam technology to deliver the responsive rush of 215 horsepower when you need it most - on the highway. The V-6 was derived from Aurora V8 technology and engineered using the most extensive computer testing in our history. The result is an aluminum construction that translates directly into enhanced driving confidence and control; the lighter weight helps handling with agility and security. And with platinum-tipped spark plugs and long-life fluids, it's even got maintenance under control.* Overall, the 3.5-liter V6 twin-cam is smoother, lighter, and swifter.  <P>The 3.5 liter V6 Intrigue engine builds on the advanced technology used for the Aurora 4.0 liter eight-cylinder engine while introducing several new features. <P>The state-of-the-art V6 engine is: <P>Powerful <BR>        -More power - 215 horsepower @ 5600 rpm <BR>        -More torque - 230 lb-ft @4400 rpm <BR>Refined <BR>        -Two-piece aluminum block minimizes noise and maximizes durability <BR>        -A new transaxle, a unique torque converter, a new phased dual chain drive and a sophisticated oil pump all work behind the scenes to reduce noise             and improve performance. <BR>Sophisticated <BR>        -An electronic shift control that makes shifting gears smooth, precise and responsive. <BR>Durable <BR>        -Extended life platinum-tipped spark plugs <BR>        -Long life Dextron III transmission fluid <BR>        -Extended life 150,000 mile engine coolant* <BR>        -A "limp-home" mode that allows the engine to run after a sudden loss of coolant. (50 miles at 50 mph) <BR>Environmental <P>        -State-of-the-art combustion and coolant systems improve emissions efficiency by allowing the engine to warm up faster and thus achieve engine              efficiency more quickly. An oil filter containment system that allows for the complete draining of engine oil and eliminates the metal filter casing for              environmentally enhanced disposal. <P>3.5 Liter V6 Engine Specifications <P>V-Angle: 90 <P>Bore x Stroke (in/mm): 89.92 mm (3.80 x 3.40 in) <P>Piston displacement: 3473 cc (211 CID) <P>Block/head material: cast aluminum <P>Compression ratio: 9.3:1 <P>Horsepower @ rpm:215 @ 5600 rpm (SAE net bhp) <P>Torque @ rpm:230 lb-ft @ 4400 rpm (SAE net lb-ft) <P>Fuel delivery: sequential fuel injection <P>Emission controls: evaporative system, catalytic converter, EGR, PCV, OBDII <P>Valvetrain: Dual overhead camshafts, hydraulic roller follower <P>Maximum engine speed: 7000 rpm <P>Special features: extended-life spark plugs, extended-life coolant, oil level sensor, coil at plug ignition, state-of-the-art combustion, two-piece aluminum block, powertrain control module, crank-triggered ignition system, advanced fuel rail design, cast aluminum pistons.<P><BR>  The 3400 Twin Dual Cam Engine<P>The 3400 Twin Dual Cam Engine is a Very High Tech upgrade of the original 2800 engine found in the Fiero.<P>A bore and stroke on the block, plus better oiling, gives a 19% increase in displacement.  This by it self would at the best give about a 15% increase in H.P. and 20% increase in torque.   Main power is from the Great improvement in Cylinder Head Design. The  4-Valve & Overhead Cam gives it a very high flow rate therefor the horsepower increase.<P>With its overhead cam design it is a Low Torque at low RPM and High Power at High RPM Engine....<P>This engine is best with the matching 284 manual 5 speed by Getrag but the Fiero 5 speed Getrag will work but not with the higher reliability of the bigger 284.<P>This engine will work well with its matching automatic but does not have great low end torque but will work well at highway speeds.<P>Key Benefits<BR> Higher Power - 215 HP. <BR> Higher Revving - 7000 RPM <BR> Lighter Weight than 2800 - Aluminum Heads & Intake <BR> Longer life engine than 2800 - Better oiling <BR> Meanest sound of any engine that we have placed in any Fiero.....!!!! <P> <BR>Has anyone tried this 284 5speed manual with a V8/3800/N*?<P>Here are some pix of a 3.4TDC in a fiero<P><BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/34-out-01.jpg">  <P><BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mvc-211x.jpg">  <P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by 87FieroGTx (edited 11-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||DKOV||11-15-2001||02:02 PM||||This is MY ingine conversion!  I mated a 3.4L TDC from a 91 Grand Prix GTP (with the GP's 284 Getrag tranny) into my 88 Fiero GT.  Some modifications including full porting/polishing especially to the intake manifolds (upper and lower), balanced, underdrive pulley, custom exhaust, K&N Custom intake, fuel presure riser/regulator, etc...<P>This motor will rip your head right off!<P>Even though this motor is normally aspirated, it pulls just like my Lotus Turbo Esprit.  Difference is, I don't have to wait for the Turbo to spool up  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I have read, several times, including on the ACE Engineering site (which is where the info posted by 87fieroGTx above came from), that the 3.4 is LOW torque at low RPMs...  From my experience, this is NOT true!<P>Sure, at 1000 PRMs (idle) it's not very torquey, but dumping the clutch at only 2500 RPMs is a RIDE!!!  And that HIGH WINDING motor... BEAUTIFUL!!!  Nearly 100 MPH in 2nd gear IS a bit high, but completely possible.<P>Mate all that high winding power to a cutom exhaust and you can't tell it from a brand new Ferrari.  It is INDEED the meanest sounding motor swap available to a Fiero.<P>Obviously, I highly recommend it!  I also recommend getting 17 inch wheels wrapped in Yokohama AVS sports just to hold the car to the ground!<P>I'll get hard numbers on the motor soon (I'm tweaking again) that I hope to show around 300 HP (Normally Aspirated w/NO NOS) and 0-60 times around 5 seconds, 0 - 100 times around 10 seconds and 1/4 times around 12.  I KNOW the car will do it, I just have to document it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>The GREAT thing is that this motor is highly reliable and a great daily driver.  Easy to run in start/stop traffic as well as flat out.  They only thing a guy has to worry about is tires  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>SERIOUSLY think about this motor (or the 3.5L)... it is a GREAT alternative.  All the extra HP without all the extra weight.  All that great sound without have to tweak to get it.  Tunable, yet reliable.<P>Gotta LOVE it!<P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000007||87FieroGTx||11-15-2001||02:32 PM|||| Sounds sweet DKOV! I'm leaning twards this swap more and more every day even know the 3.8s/c gives more power per $$$. "I" just think the fiero should have more of a high reving exotic sounding engine mated with a nice manual trans.<P> But that's just me <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P> Do you have any pictures/mp3/video's of your car?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||DKOV||11-15-2001||03:24 PM||||I don't really believe that the 3800 is better for the buck... I could be wrong.  I just can't imagine the 3800 sticking me throught he back of my seat like my 3.4 TDC.<P>Sure, with a Supercharger the 3800 will put out around 240HP but the 3.4L is 215 BONE STOCK anyway... un-charged.  Take the SC away from the 3800 (to equalize the field) and all you get is 180 HP.  A 60 HP boost in power for all the extra heat and maintenance involved with a SC'd 3800 just doesn't make much sense to me.  Espcailly when you can boost the HP of a stock 3.4L to 240+ for under $500!  260 NORMALLY ASPIRATED HP... now THAT's the ticket.  Spend a bit more and 300+ is just around the corner  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Anyway, I have only got the pic done of the swap and some external shots.  I live in the NW and it is pouring down rain 24/7 right now but I'll try and get some "action" photos ASAP.  I wish I could run my 3.4L Fiero agaist my Lotus Esprit Turbo to see how they compare...  but I can't drive BOTH cars at the same time  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Anyway, YES... I'll get lots more of "the fun stuff" very soon.  Burn outs, Lots of tire smoke, high speed passes... y'know  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Until then...<P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000009||87FieroGTx||11-15-2001||11:36 PM|||| How much did it cost to put in your car?||||reg||1
Z||000010||artherd||11-16-2001||01:05 AM||||Hey, I've got your 'other' driver!  ;D)<P>GM managed to make a couple of cammy, wide-powerband DOHC motors, and I agree, they're perfectly suited for the exotic character of our cars!!!  <P><BR>PS: the 3.5, being a Northstar that didn't drink it's milk every morning, will also BOLT RIGHT UP TO A FIERO TRANNY!!!<P>Well, with minimal mods to the tranny's bellhousing it will.<P>See my sig pic for a stock 88's Getrag bolted onto my Northstar motor.<P>The rest of the swap is just like a Northstar, only you would end up with a lighter car! <P>Not sure of the mod potential of the 3.5 (if any.  Well, I'd imagine the chrfab lifters and valve springs for a Northstar would work on your engine.  They'd probally work your heads in a similar way too.  gain maybe 75hp this way?  So you have a potential for a decent 300bhp motor here...<P>And with just lifters and valve springs, the STOCK Northstar, (and I am extrapolating here, 3.5) should be good *past 8,000rpm*<P>Eight Thousand.    <P>Viva Ferrari!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DKOV:<BR><B>I wish I could run my 3.4L Fiero agaist my Lotus Esprit Turbo to see how they compare...  but I can't drive BOTH cars at the same time   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000011||DKOV||11-16-2001||03:19 AM||||Cost for the Conversion was around $2500.00 including EVERYTHING I needed.  Even stuff that didn't come with the Conversion, like new plug wires (8.5s), new platinum plus 4s, a additional custom motor torque mount, etc.<P>The motor and tranny came together out of my 91 GTP that got toasted by a drunk, hit-n-run driver.  They totalled the car and I bought it back for $1000.00.  Snagged the motor, tranny, axles, ECU and some shifter bracketry then sold the remains for nearly $900.00.  So my motor/tranny parts really only cost me around $100.00  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>When I bought the GTP, the previous owner had already upgraded the crank and rods, stroked it a bit and had the valves done.  That aside, I've put maybe another $1000.00 into additional mods (some that I didn't need) to get past that magic 300 HP mark.<P>All in all, not a bad deal if you ask me.<P>Of course, buying this motor WITH the included tranny may run you around $2000 - $3000 bucks, plus the conversion kit at $2000 and the basic cost for an additional 50+ HP is around $500 bucks.  Less than $5000 the HARD way.  Still, that's COMPLETE and ready-to-run.<P>I spent alot of extra time replacing every little thing I seen under there too... shifter cables, hoses, suspension components (gotta see my 13 INCH Brakes!!!), poly, cradle mounts, wheels/tires, intake, CPU, etc.  I even swaped the headlamps to an HID setup!<P>All in all, I may have as much as $15,000 bucks into this car by now  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>All this just to race these past two season and convert it to a Ferrari 355 over this winter...  Glutton for punishment?  Oh yes!<P>Just remember, it CAN be done for under $5000.  You don't have to go nuts like I did.<P>As for that other driver bit...  I'll think about it in direct proportion to your "donation"   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000012||TI_3VOM||11-17-2001||04:23 PM||||!!HEY, MY HUNDRETH POST!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"><P>Well, this is for those that have done this conversion:<BR>-since the 3.4TDC/DOHC is based on the same block as the 2.8 V-6 in the Fiero, does that mean that the same motor mounts are also used?<BR>-how is the torque strut attached? Is it even used?<BR>-how easy/difficult is it to fabricate the coolant and fuel delivery lines for this swap?<BR>-"Keepin' it clean": Exhaust system routing, with cat.(p.s., I want to keep the trunk)<BR>...that's all for now. More questions later.||||reg||5
